# Summary of the Bill (H.R. 1, "One Big Beautiful Bill Act")

## Subchapter A — Termination of Green New Deal Subsidies

**Sec. 70501. Termination of previously-owned clean vehicle credit.**
This section terminates the §25E credit for previously-owned clean vehicles by moving its expiration date from December 31, 2032 to September 30, 2025. Purchases after that date will no longer qualify for the credit.

**Sec. 70502. Termination of clean vehicle credit.**
The §30D credit for new clean vehicles is terminated for vehicles acquired after September 30, 2025 (previously set to run through vehicles placed in service after December 31, 2032). Conforming amendments to §30D(e) delete the battery sourcing and critical mineral requirement provisions (clauses (v) and (iv)–(vi)) that had allowed higher credit amounts.

**Sec. 70503. Termination of qualified commercial clean vehicles credit.**
The §45W credit for qualified commercial clean vehicles is terminated by moving its expiration from December 31, 2032 to September 30, 2025. Businesses purchasing clean commercial vehicles after that date receive no credit.

**Sec. 70504. Termination of alternative fuel vehicle refueling property credit.**
The §30C credit for alternative fuel vehicle refueling property (e.g., EV chargers) is terminated by moving its expiration from December 31, 2032 to June 30, 2026. Property placed in service after that date no longer qualifies.

**Sec. 70505. Termination of energy efficient home improvement credit.**
The §25C credit for energy efficient home improvements (e.g., heat pumps, insulation) is terminated for property placed in service after December 31, 2025 (previously December 31, 2032). A conforming amendment updates the oil furnace/hot water boiler qualification to require meeting 2021 Energy Star efficiency criteria and being rated for fuel blends at least 20 percent of which consist of an eligible fuel.

**Sec. 70506. Termination of residential clean energy credit.**
The §25D credit for residential clean energy (e.g., rooftop solar) is terminated for expenditures made after December 31, 2025 (previously phasing down through 2034). Conforming amendments strike the scheduled rate reductions, leaving the 30 percent rate in place until termination.

**Sec. 70507. Termination of energy efficient commercial buildings deduction.**
A new subsection (i) is added to §179D providing that the energy efficient commercial buildings deduction does not apply to property whose construction begins after June 30, 2026. Projects starting construction on or before that date remain eligible.

**Sec. 70508. Termination of new energy efficient home credit.**
The §45L credit for contractors of new energy efficient homes is terminated by moving its expiration from December 31, 2032 to June 30, 2026. Homes acquired after that date no longer generate the credit.

**Sec. 70509. Termination of cost recovery for energy property.**
Section 168(e)(3)(B)(vi) is amended to strike subclause (I), removing certain energy property (geothermal and other qualifying property placed in the 5-year category under prior law) from 5-year MACRS depreciation treatment. The change applies to property whose construction begins after December 31, 2024.

**Sec. 70510. Modifications of zero-emission nuclear power production credit.**
Section 45U is amended to deny the zero-emission nuclear power production credit to "specified foreign entities" for taxable years beginning after enactment, and to "foreign-influenced entities" for taxable years beginning more than 2 years after enactment. The amendments apply to taxable years beginning after the date of enactment.

**Sec. 70511. Termination of clean hydrogen production credit.**
The §45V clean hydrogen production credit is terminated early by moving the construction-start deadline from January 1, 2033 to January 1, 2028. Hydrogen facilities must begin construction before 2028 to qualify.

**Sec. 70512. Termination and restrictions on clean electricity production credit.**
The §45Y clean electricity production credit is terminated for wind and solar facilities placed in service after December 31, 2027 (facilities beginning construction within 12 months of enactment retain eligibility), while other technologies continue under the existing phase-out. Facilities beginning construction after December 31, 2025 are denied the credit if they receive "material assistance from a prohibited foreign entity" — a sweeping new §7701(a)(51)–(52) regime defining specified foreign entities (e.g., foreign entities of concern, Chinese military companies, foreign-controlled entities), foreign-influenced entities (e.g., 25 percent single or 40 percent aggregate foreign ownership, 15 percent foreign-held debt, or certain licensing/control arrangements), and escalating "material assistance cost ratio" thresholds (e.g., 40 percent for facilities in 2026 rising to 60 percent after 2029; 55–75 percent for energy storage; component-specific thresholds for solar, wind, inverters, batteries, and critical minerals). The section also denies the credit for wind/solar leasing arrangements, restricts credit transfers to specified foreign entities under §6418(g), extends the statute of limitations to 6 years for material-assistance errors, imposes accuracy-related penalties (lowering the substantial-understatement threshold to 1 percent for disallowed energy credits), creates a new §6695B supplier-certification penalty (greater of 10 percent of underpayment or $5,000), expands nuclear energy community treatment for advanced nuclear facilities, and directs Treasury to issue safe harbor tables by December 31, 2026.

**Sec. 70513. Termination and restrictions on clean electricity investment credit.**
The §48E clean electricity investment credit is terminated for wind and solar facilities placed in service after December 31, 2027 (with an exception for energy storage technology placed in service at such facilities), applying to facilities beginning construction more than 12 months after enactment. Facilities and energy storage technology beginning construction after December 31, 2025 are denied the credit if they include material assistance from a prohibited foreign entity, and the credit is denied to specified and foreign-influenced entities, with a new 100 percent recapture under §50(a)(4) for applicable payments to prohibited foreign entities within 10 years of placement in service. The section also denies the credit for wind/solar leasing arrangements, revises domestic content thresholds (45 percent for construction starting on or after June 16, 2025 and before 2026, 50 percent in 2026, 55 percent thereafter; offshore wind at 27.5/35 percent), reduces the §48 energy percentage for certain energy property from 2 percent to 0 percent, and applies the §48E credit to qualified fuel cell property at a flat 30 percent for construction beginning after December 31, 2025.

**Sec. 70514. Phase-out and restrictions on advanced manufacturing production credit.**
The §45X advanced manufacturing production credit is modified so that a "sale" of an integrated component requires at least 65 percent of the secondary component's total direct material costs to be attributable to U.S.-mined, produced, or manufactured primary components (effective for components sold in taxable years beginning after December 31, 2026). Applicable critical minerals (other than metallurgical coal) phase out at 75 percent in 2031, 50 percent in 2032, 25 percent in 2033, and 0 percent thereafter; wind energy components terminate after December 31, 2027; and metallurgical coal terminates after December 31, 2029. Metallurgical coal suitable for steel production is added as an applicable critical mineral with a 2.5 percent credit rate, battery module definitions are expanded to include essential equipment like current collector assemblies and voltage sense harnesses, and the credit is denied to specified and foreign-influenced entities and for components with material assistance from prohibited foreign entities.

**Sec. 70515. Restriction on the extension of advanced energy project credit program.**
Section 48C(e)(3)(C) is amended so that the $10 billion allocation ceiling for the advanced energy project credit program shall not be increased. This takes effect on the date of enactment, capping the program at its existing amount.

## Subchapter B — Enhancement of America-First Energy Policy

**Sec. 70521. Extension and modification of clean fuel production credit.**
The §45Z clean fuel production credit is extended from December 31, 2027 to December 31, 2029, but is restricted to transportation fuel exclusively derived from feedstocks produced or grown in the United States, Mexico, or Canada (effective for fuel produced after December 31, 2025). Emissions rates may not be negative (except for animal-manure-derived fuels, which may receive negative rates), indirect land use change emissions are excluded from rate calculations, and the sustainable aviation fuel special rate is eliminated in favor of the standard $1.00 amount. The section also terminates the §6426(k) sustainable aviation fuel credit for sales or use after September 30, 2025, doubles the small agri-biodiesel producer credit from 10 to 20 cents per gallon (through December 31, 2026, coordinated with §45Z), denies the credit to specified and foreign-influenced entities, and prevents double credits for fuels already claiming §45Z.

**Sec. 70522. Restrictions on carbon oxide sequestration credit.**
The §45Q carbon oxide sequestration credit is denied to specified foreign entities and foreign-influenced entities for taxable years beginning after enactment. The section establishes parity among uses of qualified carbon oxide — storage, use as a tertiary injectant in enhanced oil/gas recovery, and utilization under §45Q(f)(5) — with a base credit amount of $17 (inflation-adjusted after 2026, substituting 2025 for 1990) and $36 for facilities not meeting certain conditions. These changes apply to facilities or equipment placed in service after enactment.

**Sec. 70523. Intangible drilling and development costs taken into account for purposes of computing adjusted financial statement income.**
Section 56A(c)(13) (the AFSI adjustment for oil and gas companies under the corporate alternative minimum tax) is amended to allow reduction of AFSI by both §167 depreciation on §168 property and §263(c) intangible drilling and development cost deductions, to the extent allowed in computing taxable income. Correspondingly, the adjustment disregards depreciation and depletion expense taken into account on the applicable financial statement for such property. This applies to taxable years beginning after December 31, 2025, benefiting oil and gas producers.

**Sec. 70524. Income from hydrogen storage, carbon capture, advanced nuclear, hydropower, and geothermal energy added to qualifying income of certain publicly traded partnerships.**
Section 7704(d)(1)(E) is expanded so that publicly traded partnerships can treat as "qualifying income" (avoiding entity-level corporate tax) income and gains from transportation or storage of hydrogen (liquefied or compressed) and various fuels, carbon capture at qualified facilities where at least 50 percent of carbon oxide production is qualified carbon oxide, electricity from advanced nuclear facilities, electricity or thermal energy from hydropower or geothermal resources (§45(c)(1)(D) or (H)), and operation of specified energy property under §48(a)(3)(A)(iii) or (vii). This applies to taxable years beginning after December 31, 2025.

**Sec. 70525. Allow for payments to certain individuals who dye fuel.**
A new §6435 allows the Secretary to pay persons (without interest) the §4081 tax previously paid on diesel fuel or kerosene that was removed from a terminal as eligible indelibly dyed fuel (i.e., tax-paid fuel later dyed and exempt under §4082(a)). Conforming amendments extend registration, claims, and excessive-claim penalty rules (§§6206, 6430, 6675) to these payments. The provision applies to dyed fuel removed on or after the date that is 180 days after enactment.

## Subchapter C — Other Reforms

**Sec. 70531. Modifications to de minimis entry privilege for commercial shipments.**
Section 321 of the Tariff Act of 1930 (the "de minimis" exemption) is amended to impose a civil penalty of up to $5,000 for the first violation and up to $10,000 for each subsequent violation on persons who use the de minimis privilege to import articles in violation of U.S. customs law, effective 30 days after enactment. The commercial shipment exception to the $800 de minimis threshold is repealed, effective July 1, 2027, meaning commercial shipments will no longer qualify for duty-free de minimis treatment.

## Chapter 6 — Enhancing Deduction and Income Tax Credit Guardrails, and Other Reforms

**Sec. 70601. Modification and extension of limitation on excess business losses of noncorporate taxpayers.**
The §461(l) limitation on excess business losses of noncorporate taxpayers (currently scheduled to expire after 2028) is made permanent, applying to taxable years beginning after December 31, 2026. The inflation-adjustment baselines are updated from 2018/2017 to 2025/2024, applying to taxable years beginning after December 31, 2025.

**Sec. 70602. Treatment of payments from partnerships to partners for property or services.**
Section 707(a)(2) is amended by replacing the phrase "Under regulations prescribed" with "Except as provided," narrowing the scope of regulations governing when payments between partnerships and partners are treated as though made between unrelated parties. The amendment applies to services performed and property transferred after enactment, with an express rule of construction that no inference is created regarding treatment of payments on or before enactment.

**Sec. 70603. Excessive employee remuneration from controlled group members and allocation of deduction.**
Section 162(m) is amended to extend the $1 million deduction limitation on publicly held corporations' employee remuneration across controlled groups (under §414(b), (c), (m), or (o)): if aggregate remuneration from all group members to a "specified covered employee" exceeds $1 million, each member's deduction is limited to an "allocable limitation amount" proportional to its share of that employee's remuneration. This applies to taxable years beginning after December 31, 2025, closing a loophole where affiliated companies each paid up to $1 million to the same executive.

**Sec. 70604. Excise tax on certain remittance transfers.**
A new subchapter C of chapter 36 imposes a 1 percent excise tax on remittance transfers, paid by the sender and collected and remitted quarterly by the remittance transfer provider (with secondary provider liability if uncollected). The tax applies only to transfers funded with cash, money orders, cashier's checks, or similar physical instruments, and does not apply to transfers funded from accounts at U.S. financial institutions subject to Bank Secrecy Act requirements or with U.S.-issued debit or credit cards. It applies to transfers made after December 31, 2025.

**Sec. 70505/70605. Enforcement provisions with respect to COVID-related employee retention credits.**
COVID-ERTC promoters (generally, persons whose ERTC-related gross receipts exceed 20 percent of total receipts with contingent fees, or 50 percent without, or 20 percent plus $500,000; excluding certified professional employer organizations) face a $1,000 assessable penalty per failure to comply with due diligence requirements similar to §6695(g), applying to aid, assistance, or advice provided after enactment. No ERTC credit or refund is allowed after enactment unless the claim was filed on or before January 31, 2024, and the statute of limitations for assessing ERTC amounts is extended to 6 years after the latest of the original return filing, deemed filing, or claim date (with matching extension for wage-deduction refunds). Section 6676's erroneous-claim penalty is expanded to cover employment tax as well as income tax claims.

**Sec. 70606. Social security number requirement for American Opportunity and Lifetime Learning credits.**
Section 25A(g) is amended to deny the American Opportunity and Lifetime Learning credits unless the taxpayer includes their own Social Security number, the name and SSN of any student other than the taxpayer or spouse, and — for the American Opportunity Tax Credit — the employer identification number of each educational institution paid. Omissions are treated as mathematical or clerical errors correctable without deficiency procedures under §6213(g)(2)(J). This applies to taxable years beginning after December 31, 2025, targeting improper claims by taxpayers without valid SSNs.

**Sec. 70607. Task force on the replacement of Direct File.**
The section appropriates $15 million from the Treasury for fiscal year 2026 (available until September 30, 2026) for the Department of the Treasury to deliver a report to Congress within 90 days of enactment on replacing the IRS Direct File program. The report must address the cost of establishing public-private partnerships providing free tax filing for up to 70 percent of taxpayers by adjusted gross income, taxpayer preferences between government-run and private free filing, feasibility of a consistent multi-provider approach, and the cost of developing and running a free direct e-file system including differential coverage options. [end of text]


